
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ old array contains x 

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array contains x at index 

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array IndexOf x is greater or equal to old array IndexOf x 
          // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array LastIndexOf x is greater or equal to old array LastIndexOf x 
          // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array contains x 
	    Assumption.Holds(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
          // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array contains x 
          // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array lastIndexOf x >= old array lastIndexOf x  
        }
    }
}

In this example, PexAssumeUnderTest and PexObserve are used to track the state of the ArrayList before and after the Insert method is called, as well as to observe the return value of those methods. 

Preconditions, assertions, and assumptions are used to specify in which states the methods should act their specified ways. PexAssumeUnderTest is used to specify that the ArrayList should be an under-test object, meaning that Pex and the team that is testing this class should be aware that it is under test for testing and it does not interact with the rest of the system. 

This example is based on a previous problem, "ArrayList: Insert method" where you had to test different inputs with the Insert method. This is similar, but instead of testing a method, you are testing an ArrayList class, and are finding edge cases and potential bugs.

I hope this helps you understand how to use Pex to find potential issues with code, or how to find edge cases in your code.

