As discussed in the last section, Pex is a powerful tool used primarily for automatic generation of unit tests. The tool uses static analysis and knowledge of a method's code to generate tests. This process focuses on potential paths through the code, or branches, which the tool will explore, looking for possible test cases.

Pex works by iteratively extending the test cases generated by adding additional method calls to the current path. The Pex engine maintains a set of potential paths through the code, or branches. As it creates test cases and extends them, it checks to see if there are any conditions that could be true or false.

As it continues to extend paths, it analyzes the conditions to determine if an alternative is reached that is worth exploring. The Pex engine uses a variety of analysis techniques, including--------------------            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= index);

