As your contract tests become more complex, your test code will typically become more verbose and hard to maintain. Furthermore, using `AssumePrecondition` can sometimes prevent the testing framework from discovering possible test cases that are incorrectly assumed; these are known as flaky tests. Finally, keep in mind that the `PexObserve` statements are just placeholders that would need to be replaced with the actual parameters or values being tested.

For more information, see [Code Contracts and the .NET Runtime](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/using-code-coverage-to-measure-the-quality-of-your-code?view=vs-2017).

