This is a sample solution. The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute allows the Pex explorer to be aware of which objects and their states are part of the "undertest" state, and PexInjection assumes that the test case should be injected with certain values. The PexObserve.ValueForViewing method is used to log the pre- and post-conditions of a method. The method InsertContract in a theoretical ArrayList class may be inserted in the partial class. The PexObserve.ValueForViewing method generates output that makes it possible to verify that the test is behaving as expected. 10 other methods are used inside the sample to make the solution functional and valid.

In this code template, you will find examples of how to write unit tests for your code using the Postcondition (Assert) and Precondition annotations. Testing with these styles is useful for verifying whether the conditions that should be true before and after a method are indeed true.

The [PexMethod] attribute used in this code is one of the ways to mark methods which are to be included in the exploration of paths through a code under test by Pex.

The [PexClass] attribute indicates the entry point for generating test cases. This attribute can be used at the namespace level, class level, or assembly level. The PexClass attribute lets you specify the entry point for Pex-generated tests.

This is a simple framework for unit testing. In real software unit testing, there are other aspects like Isolated Unit Testing, Mocking, and Separation of concerns that must be taken care of.
```csharp
namespace Solution
{
    public class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            int[] arr = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
            TestRunner runner = new TestRunner();
            runner.RunTest(arr);
        }
    }

    public class ArrayList
    {
        private int[] arr;

        public void Insert(int index, int value)
        {
            // implementation details omitted
        }

        public int Count { get { return arr.Length; } }
    }

    public class TestRunner
    {
        public void RunTest(int[] arr)
        {
            foreach (int i in arr)
            {
                ArrayList arrayList = new ArrayList();
                arrayList.Insert(0, i);
                Assert(arrayList.Count == 1);
            }
        }

        private void Assert(bool condition)
        {
            if (!condition)
            {
                throw new Exception("Assertion failed.");
            }
        }
    }
}
```
